PART I: WHAT THE CLIMB TAKES

Gear had made his way back up alone, the long way, clinging to whatever in him answers to buried metal, and nobody had thought to look for him until he was already most of the way there. He didn't say much about it. Ismara built him a pair of little ice skis to ride instead of the single ball he balances on, and pulled him along for a while before anyone thought better of it — a jetpack, someone suggested, and got nothing but silence back, the kind an idea earns when nobody in the room has the first idea what a jetpack is.

The mountain kept taking what it always takes. Everybody rolled again, and it landed unevenly, the way it always does: Saijah aged five. The rest of the company drifted apart from each other by smaller margins, no two of them keeping the same pace through it. Bjorn's roll landed the same as everyone else's, and nothing came of it — no year added, nothing to see in his face at all. Mila, ten now, sat close to the fire and said her legs felt stretchy, and left it there.

Ismara went looking for Bjorn again, the same question she'd already put to him once on this same climb: what are you. He still didn't have an answer. He never has. Mable was greyer than she'd been at the bottom of the Steps, favoring her front left leg over her right, old in a way that wasn't going to reverse. Saijah asked whether a grand healing spell could do anything for the leg and was told plainly that it doesn't work that way — a healing spell forces the body's own repair along faster, replaces damaged tissue with new, and arthritis isn't damage, it's an absence there's nothing left to force. She couldn't fix it. She gave Mable a honey lollipop instead, and a portion of the last of the salted meat, and some of the grain that had survived the trip better than anything else in the cart — the cold had turned the mead strange and thick with age and left most of the rest of the stores rotten, but salt-cured meat holds, and grain keeps.

PART II: THE SECOND STAIRWAY

Saijah went looking on her own — a white shape she'd seen before, watching from a distance, the same presence that had crossed her path once already in the foothills after Mzinchaleft. She followed it down a deer path, across a small creek, around a bend, and found a second stairway cut into the mountain's own face — not carved, not built, simply formed, the rock standing up in steps the way basalt sometimes stands on its own.

Before she went far enough to lose the rest of the company entirely, she called a familiar to carry word back — she had never done it before, hadn't known the gift was hers to reach for, and what answered was a gull, pale and wind-worn, already circling her before she'd finished asking. She named her Petunia and sent her back to the others with the one thing that mattered: she was following the stag, she was safe, and she'd find her way back to them.

The presence she was following spoke to her as she climbed, and it did not answer plainly. She pressed it — whether this had to do with the Elder Scroll, whether it was the dragons, whether it was Alduin's own working reaching backward through time — and each time it told her she was close and then that she wasn't, that she was warmer, then that she was warmer in the wrong direction. What it confirmed, eventually: two things, both left unaddressed by the company so far, had come together here to make this. Not abstract. Present. One of them was her. The Throat of the World is a pillar, one of several holding the world up, and something down at its root is cutting into it — that much it gave her outright. What was colliding with it, and why it had chosen her specifically to carry the weight of finding out, it would not say. Follow it far enough, it told her, and she'd likely have the rest of it worked out before she reached the summit.

A waystone stood partway up, older than anything she'd found on the marked Steps below — written in Atmoran, a script none of them had a ready way into. She worked it alone, without help, the mountain's weather charging her for every hour she spent bent over the stone the same way it charges everyone else: a year here, a handful there, one roll costing her nothing and the next taking five years at once. She finished it anyway.

It read: before the tongue had a house of stone, the root spoke first. Here the mountain listened before it was taught to. The Dragon took worship and called it due. The Hawk taught the height and is remembered gentler than she was. The Snake, the Moth, the Owl, the Whale, the Bear, the Fox — each had a face and a season, and each is mostly forgotten now. The Wolf's face went unclaimed when the rest went to their new names. It answers still, this door beneath us — to every voice bent to one need, never to one voice alone. Those who asked it for less than they meant did not get to ask twice.

It told her plainly what it was: a door existed somewhere beneath the summit, older than the tradition High Hrothgar keeps, opened only by a company speaking together in true need. It gave her nothing about where that door stood.

PART III: THE CHOICE, MADE TOGETHER

She came back and laid it all out for the rest of them — the stair, the stone, what it said, what she still didn't understand about why it had come to her instead of anyone else. The company weighed it as a whole: the marked Steps, known, likely to mean more cultists standing in the way; or the second stairway, unknown except for what a god had chosen to tell one of them and nobody else. They chose to go together. Varon had already gone ahead on his own errand and couldn't be reached — Petunia was sent after him and came back without him; whatever he was doing, he was doing it too well to be found by a bird.

The climb up the second stairway ran past carving after carving, each keeping the pattern the first stone had promised: the totems, one after another, worked into the rock alongside living things that matched them, far larger than anything the same species grows to elsewhere — caribou taller than Ismara standing at a distance, foxes rising to shoulder height. Ylva went quiet in front of all of it, quieter than any of them had ever seen her, and stood a long while before a massive carved fox with her eyes closed and her hands empty at her sides. They took etchings of every carving they passed, meaning to carry the record with them even without understanding all of it yet. Partway up, a hard-won roll finally broke Saijah's migraine loose — four months of it, cut off in one instant, gone long enough for her to remember what its absence felt like.

PART IV: THE STAIRWAY REJOINS, AND THE TROLL

The second stairway ran back into the marked Steps before it reached anything resembling a summit, folding the company back onto the same path the rest of Skyrim's pilgrims have always climbed — no shortcut past what waited ahead, only a different road to the same door. And what waited ahead was the troll.

They tried to go around it. It noticed a small sound and started toward it before the sound had finished — Muffle went up fast enough to cut it off. The company debated in place while it worked. Nora held a fireball ready and didn't need it yet. Orion laid Muffle over the whole group. Somehow, between them, invisibility covered everyone as well.

Ismara used telekinesis to push Bjorn and Mable clear of the ground the troll was working, and the push left a trail behind them in the snow — the one thing none of the spellwork could hide. The troll found the trail and followed it to where it ended, and swiped once, at the two who'd made it.

It rolled a natural one. It hit them anyway, a single blow landing across the same ground Bjorn and Mable both occupied — far more than whatever Mable had left after everything the climb had already aged out of her. She did not survive it.

Bjorn went to her in the snow and put a hand flat against her neck, and stayed there a moment. He said nothing. Nobody asked him to move.

PART V: WHAT WAS LEFT AFTER

The company didn't turn to fight. They threw what they had to break the scent the troll was working from — a scattered decoy, a lure rattled and thrown clear to draw its attention somewhere else — and it worked well enough to buy the distance they needed. They got clear of the den, Mable left behind them in the snow, and climbed the rest of the way to High Hrothgar with the fight still owed and unpaid.

PART VI: HIGH HROTHGAR, AND THE DOOR THAT WAITS BEHIND THE TROLL

Arngeir met them at the door and recognized Klimmek's pack on sight before he asked who any of them were. He heard out everything they had to say about Ivarstead, about the dying below, about what they'd already done and what they were asking the Greybeards to do about it, and answered the way the order always answers: the Voice is not for this. Not a torn order weighing a hard call — a closed one. They asked, in every way the room could think to ask it, and got the same answer back each time.

What he gave them instead was the shape of what's actually happening under the mountain. More than one Tower holds the world up; the Throat is only the one directly beneath this monastery, and something is cutting into its root from below. A door leads down to it, sealed, old, and it stands inside the very den the troll has been guarding the whole time the company has been trying to get past it rather than through it. Opening it takes the company standing together at that threshold, all of them, speaking as one, in true need — the same mechanism the second stairway's own stone had already promised.

The Greybeards noticed something about Bjorn without naming it: he hasn't aged a day through any of this, the same distortion that's cost the rest of the company years apiece leaving him entirely untouched. They said only that it was strange. They asked nothing further, and offered nothing further, and the company let it sit exactly where it landed.

They traded what they had for what the monastery could spare — dried mushrooms and snowberries handed over for a run of potions, healing draughts and FP restoratives set aside from years of pilgrim offerings nobody here has any use for. The company left with more of both than they'd arrived with, the troll's den still standing between them and the door, the fight still to be had.
